% EMNLP 2026 Main — ARR May 2026
% Generational Depth Contamination
\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[review]{acl}
\usepackage{times}
\usepackage{latexsym}
\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage{microtype}
\usepackage{graphicx}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{booktabs}
\usepackage{colortbl}
\usepackage{multirow}
\usepackage{enumitem}
\usepackage{subcaption}
\usepackage[section]{placeins}

\newcommand{\Kstar}{{K^*}}
\newcommand{\Kempirical}{{K^*_{\text{empirical}}}}
\newcommand{\Ktheory}{{K^*_{\text{theory}}}}
\newcommand{\auc}{\text{AUC}}
\newcommand{\jsd}{\text{JSD}}

\title{Generational Depth Estimation of Recursive Synthetic Text:\\Measuring the Discrimination Boundary}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

% === Abstract ===
\begin{abstract}
Web-scale corpora are increasingly contaminated by recursive synthetic content, yet no method estimates a document's generational depth.
We measure a lower bound on the generational discrimination boundary, $\Kempirical$ (the maximum depth at which adjacent generations remain distinguishable), using 15 statistical features and an OBD-LightGBM classifier.
Under prompt-chain generation across 10 model configurations (6 architecture families, 1B--8B), 3 source domains, and 4 decoding strategies, for web text (C4, Wikipedia), $\Kempirical \leq 2$ regardless of model family or scale ($\theta{=}0.60$); across all ten configurations, $\Kempirical \leq 3$, with structured academic text (arXiv) reaching $\Kempirical{=}3$ across two architectures.
Among the six nominal $\Kempirical{=}2$ configurations, four retain significance under Bonferroni correction; the two marginal cases reflect narrow effect sizes rather than classification failure.
A single generation pass suffices to render general web text indistinguishable from deeper synthetic content, a \emph{one-wash laundering} effect.
Model-free statistical features enable 80--100\% cross-model transfer with scorer-invariant feature rankings ($\rho > 0.90$), supporting depth estimation on corpora from unknown generators without retraining.
As a consistency check, domain-appropriate $\Kempirical$ correctly predicts that binary filtering outperforms graduated alternatives for web text when contamination causes detectable quality loss.
\end{abstract}

% === Section 1: Introduction ===
\input{introduction}

% === Section 2: Related Work ===
\input{related_work}

% === Section 3: Method ===
\input{method}

% === Section 4: Experiments ===
\input{experiments_c1}

% === Section 5: K*-Aware Data Filtering ===
\input{experiments_c3}

% === Section 6: Discussion ===
\input{discussion}

% === Section 7: Conclusion ===
\input{conclusion}

% === Limitations (ACL required, unnumbered) ===
\section*{Limitations}

Our study has three main limitations.
(1)~\emph{Scope}: we evaluate models up to 8B parameters across 6 architecture families, English text only (C4, Wikipedia, arXiv), and prompt-chain generation; retrain-chain dynamics may yield different boundaries.
(2)~\emph{Feature set}: the 15 statistical features provide a lower bound on $\Ktheory$; richer representations (e.g., neural probes) can extend $\Kempirical$ by one additional depth for some architectures.
(3)~\emph{Threshold sensitivity}: $\Kempirical$ varies with the decision threshold $\theta$, ranging from 2--3 at $\theta{=}0.55$ to 1 at $\theta{=}0.70$; two of ten configurations are marginal at our reference $\theta{=}0.60$.

% === Ethics Statement (ACL required, unnumbered) ===
\section*{Ethics Statement}

Generational depth estimation is intended for data curation and corpus auditing, positive-sum applications that improve training data quality.
Our experiments use the publicly available C4 corpus and do not involve personally identifiable information.
A potential dual-use concern is that depth estimation techniques could inform adversarial strategies to evade detection (e.g., by calibrating rewriting to mimic earlier-generation statistics); we note that this risk is shared by all detection research and that the binary detection boundary ($\auc \geq 0.96$ at 0v1) remains robust.
Code and data will be released upon acceptance.

\bibliography{references}

\clearpage
